        "original_id": "518bda30-8df4-55f5-a143-45f6da4f1f00",
        "text": "This chunk is about:\n- People: Onesimus, Cotton Mather\n- Places: Boston, Salem\n- Concepts: slavery, inoculation, smallpox, hybridization, Salem witch trials\n\nFacts:\n- In the late 1600s and 1700s, Onesimus was an African-born man held as a slave by Cotton Mather in Boston.\n- During a smallpox outbreak in Boston, Onesimus helped mitigate the outbreak’s impact by introducing Cotton Mather to the principle of inoculation.\n- Cotton Mather promoted inoculation for disease prevention.\n- Cotton Mather also left a scientific legacy through his hybridization experiments.\n- Cotton Mather is most frequently remembered today for his vigorous support for the Salem witch trials.",
        "type": "TextSummary"
      },
      {
